         "2025-01-15T12:37:05.854-08:00\n**Attendees:** Ben pruden, Mo Sarwat, Juan, Ana, Anson\n\nDate: 01.15.25\n\n**TL;DR**: Still early in their product and commercialization process; likely more complimentary with Felt than competitive. Trying to become a more geospatial-friendly version of Databricks/other major data hosters. Mo seems strong.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\n**Overview/ Intros**\n\n* Energize introduction\n\n* Mo\n  \n  * Co-founder / CEO at Whereobots\n  \n  * CS Faculty Member at ASU\n* Ben\n  \n  * GTM Leader; been with company since march last year\n  \n  * Open-source / open-core experience for 5.5-6 years; salesforce thereafter\n\n**Genesis of Whereobots**\n\n* 2022 start with cofounder \\[Jia?\\]\n\n* Looked at the market and observed there was no data platform out there that took advantage of the advancements of data processing / management\n\n* Focus has been on generic data, put data into tables; slice and dice, etc.\n  \n  * Snowflake / Databricks / Oracle -- none of them treat the physical world data appropriately\n  \n  * Some don\\'t even have functionality for physical data storage / management\n  \n  * User is stuck implementing functions / metadata about the physical world within their own applications\n* Vision:\n  \n  * Almost all data collected is done from the physical world\n    \n    * IoT sensors, satellites, etc. collected on a second-by-second data\n    \n      * Wherobots wants to provide a platform for users to drive insights out of this data easily, at scale, and with minimal fricton\n  \n  * Treat the physical world as a \\\"first class citizen\\\" so users can build applications around this data\n\n**Product / Business Segments**\n\n* Solutions applying back in the day involved\n  \n  * A) Using a standard data platform (Snowflake) and then build entire geoprocessing system within the platform\n    \n    * But this is not efficient, scalable, or easy to maintain\n    \n    * User ends up with a dilemma\n  * B) Use ESRI for geospatial analytics, and suffer with ESRI\\'s data management / processing capabilities\n    \n    * Siloed as a technology; very old school\n    \n    * Great geospatial functionality inside; but doesn\\'t integrate\n* Wherobots is bridging the gap -- pushing geospatial awareness inside data platform so users don\\'t have\n\n* He was doing research that led to Apache Sedona\n  \n  * Pushed prototype on GitHub -- traction was ridiculous\n  \n  * Software downloaded 40 million times (cumulative)\n* Can\\'t commercialize Apache Sedona, but want to create a product around it\n\n* Seamlessly integrate data pipeline is the goal\n\n**ESRI Dethroning**\n\n* There are different approaches to driving value from geospatial data\n  \n  * F500 companies, startups, consultancies\n  \n  * A few user types\n  \n  * 1) Some of these teams are \\\"pure GIS\\\" -- team members went to school for ArcGIS; don\\'t know anything beyond that\n    \n    * They have problems with it, but well entrenched\n    \n      * 2) Traditional data engineer / data scientists that aren\\'t GIS people, but are computer / data scientists\n    \n    * Very good with data tools; understand databases and processing, but have use cases for geospatial\n    \n    * Insurance company, transportation, etc.\n  * 3) Third user is a user of both!\n  \n  * Easier to sell to the latter two than the first one\n* The good news is that selling to 2-3 is also where a lot of money is / more capable people\n  \n  * Want to target 1, but focusing more on 2-3 to start\n  \n  * *So the vision is not to build the new ESRI, but rather to build the new Databricks for geospatial?*\n  \n  * Exactly\n  \n  * *As you\\'re thinking about other physical-first datapoints, why start with geospatial, and how do you expand?*\n  \n  * There are some time-series analysis data platforms that exist out there\n  \n  * The area that\\'s lacking is geospatial, but it\\'s also ubiquitous\n  \n  * Structurally you can incorporate time-based data into geospatial\n  \n  * Support \\\"Geospatial + X\\\" data where X is traditional data variables, images, time-series data, etc.\n  \n  * Everything happens \\\"somewhere\\\" and we want to make sure the \\\"somewhere\\\" is captured\n\n**Pricing Given Open-Source**\n\n* They have innovation in Sedona and an engine the runs Sedona code very efficiently\n\n* One of the big innovation is also in their product and packaging / delivery\n\n* When starting Wherobots, version of the product that built and automated tool to deploy Sedona into their own cloud environment\n  \n  * After building that, threw it in the trash\n  \n  * Provide a fully provisioned compute for geospatial data\n  \n  * To use Wherobots: have to 1) use it in the cloud, and 2) write code for the applications you\\'re building on it\n  \n  * Have an engine for taking code and compiling your code in a very efficient way\n\n* Know that Wherobots is not in a silo; geospatial is a component\n  \n  * Integrated with Airflow, Databricks / other data pipeline tools; incorporate it as part of a larger data workflow\n  \n  * Can write it into ESRI / QGIS\n  \n  * Removed the hurdle of maintaining compute infrastructure\n* Pricing based on compute\n  \n  * Any company can decide to charge based on compute; rent an EC2 cluster in AWS and charge extra money on top; but it\\'s not that easy!\n  \n  * Don\\'t believe they charge for compute until you know what they\\'re doing -- optimize compute internally that even with profit margin added, it\\'s still way cheaper than any other platform that exists out there for the customer; large profit margin\n  \n  * 90\\% GM today\n\n**Customers**\n\n* *Is it usually large established enterprises building something new, or where can we help introduce?*\n  \n  * Ben is leading ICP definition\n  \n  * 1) Geospatial data producers - \\\"Horizon 1\\\"\n    \n    * Collecting lot of geospatial data and producing unified database / sell that data to people\n    \n    * Incorporate all land parcels and sell data to RE developers\n    \n    * POI data, develop maps\n    \n    * Focused on this segment first - given product status, this was the easiest to sell to, and realize value early on\n  * 2) Large Enterprise \\\"Horizon 2\\\" -- Infrastructure, Energy, Real Estate, Telecom, Transportation, and Insurance\n    \n    * Some are using Apache Sedona today, but avoided in the MVP because of expectations around SOC2 / Security measures\n    \n    * Fixing holes in product so it\\'s ready\n    \n    * Renewable energy infra has been high focus; SF office is at Climate Tech Hub (Nine Zero) -- did that intentionally\n    \n    * Lot of shared interest in that network -- would love to meet if in town\n  * Doing stuff of SF Climate week for a few months; planning workshops there\n\n**Competition**\n\n* *What\\'s stopping Databricks from doing this*\n  \n  * A lot of users are using Sedona in databricks\n  \n  * Same people do it with their own platforms; multiple reasons why these platforms are not investing as heavily into this:\n    \n    * 1) GenAI -- data platforms are focused on the arms race of who will win GenAI, and investments going there\n    \n    * 2) Few people on the planet that know both Geospatial + data AI ecosystem in the other; few technical people!\n  * Collectively preventing some existing data platforms from building a product that represents this first class citizen\n    \n    * Trying to increase the exposure / purview of geospatial data\n    \n    \n**Traction / Economics**\n\n* *Where are you guys now?*\n  \n  * Product is still in infancy\n  \n  * Up until 2024, buildout phase -- building core team and expanding product MVP\n  \n  * Also selling simultaneously -- have about 12 paying customers today; small customers, but working with them closely -- run workflows in Wherobots, report bugs, etc.\n\n**Fundraise**\n\n* *What\\'s the objective of this meeting?*\n  \n  * For them, been looking at this space for a long time:\n  \n  * *1) Get to know you / learn more about the product*\n    \n    * Going to make an investment in GIS space / ecosystem\n    \n      * *2) Seeing how you\\'re thinking about Fundraising*\n    \n    * Just raised, so not looking to raise now\n    \n    * But want to work with partners who believe in the vision\n    \n    * Next round expectations + milestones\n      \n      * Have MVP now customers are using, but want to grow from 12 customers to 50-60 (YE2025)\n      \n      * Revenue milestones / MAUs: status of product itself\n      \n      * **September this year** --> get started then on a round; exception for Energize\n  * *3) Commercialization support*\n    \n    * Best way to develop value for our investors is to scale GTM for the companies we invest in\n    \n    * *How can Energize help?*\n  \n  * If you can connect us to potential customers in the domain we\\'ve discussed, penetrate teams\n  \n  * Not every introduction will be a commercial relationship, but could lead to something potentially; help study Wherobots from a commercial perspective\n* Follow up to share materials\n2025-01-15T09:52:35.729-08:00\nAttendees: Mo (CEO), Ben (GTM lead), Juan, Anson, Ana\n\n\n\nTLDR: Still early, but is the team that built Apache Sedona. Building the next generation database for managing physical world data (i.e., geospatial). Already work with Felt, but the idea is to sell this compute and capability to enterprises working with geospatial data in new ways. Asked for customer intros, but generally KIT.\n\n\n\nNotes:\n\n* Overview\n  \n  * Mo - co-founder and CEO of Wherobots, before faculty at Arizona\n  \n  * Ben - lead GTM efforts... been with the company since March of the last year\n  \n  * Started Wherobots in the second half of 2022, back in the time it was me and my co-founder Jia\n  \n  * We looked at the market, there was no data platform that exists out there that takes into account or treats the physical world as a first class citizen within the platform itself\n  \n  * There is fantastic advancement in data processing\n  \n  * The focus has been on generic data, putting data into tables, slice and dice the data\n  \n  * Snowflake, Databricks -- none of them treats the physical world, which is a big aspect of any data we collect today\n  \n  * Some of them don\\'t even have functionality for it\n  \n  * Vision for us is that most of the data we collect\n  \n  * All of this data so many signals that we collect on a second by second basis... our vision at Wherobots is we want to provide a platform for users to drive value out of this data\n  \n  * And the solutions people were applying back then before wherobots\n  \n  * Going to use a data platform like Snowflake, cloud data platform, and I will build our entire geospatial platofrm in our applications\n  \n  * Snowflake and all of these are good platforms\n  \n  * Problem with DIY solution is that it ends up being not efficient, scalable, easy to maintain... user ends up inefficient\n  \n  * People use tools like ESRI to do the geospatial analysis\n  \n  * The problem is that they don\\'t have data management or processing application\n  \n  * User ends up also with the same problems, completely siloed stck that runs in something in the 90s\n  \n  * Yet... it has good geospatial functionality, doesn\\'t integrate with modern data stack\n  \n  * Wherobots is bridging the gap\n  \n  * The reason we started Wherobots,wasn\\'t like we were sitting in a bar and just came up with this\n  \n  * Did a lot of research on these two schools of thought and came up with a prototype back then led to software called Apache Sedona\n  \n  * We came up with the prototype, pushed it on Github, thought maybe 5-10 people would start interacting with it\n  \n  * Traction has been amazing... software has been downloaded more than 40M times\n  \n  * So there ... that proves there is a gap in the market and people are thirsty for software like this to bridge the gap i described earlier\n  \n  * Sitting on a gold mine here... want to build an ecosystem around it, make geospatial data easy\n* Product\n  \n  * How you going to disrupt the current approach\n    \n    * Teams exist at fortune 500 companies, startups, etc.\n    \n    * Some of these teams are pure GIS... team members went to school for ArcGIS, know nothing beyond this\n    \n    * Have problems with it but trying to put band-aids on and move on\n    \n    * This is the first type of user\n    \n    * The second type of user is traditional data scientists in fortune 500 compnaies\n    \n    * Can be computer scientists, data scientists... very good with data tools\n    \n    * Understand data processing, algorithms, but they have use cases for geospatial\n    \n    * These could be people working in insurance companies, automotive company, etc.\n    \n    * They know nothing about ESRI and when they take a look at ESRI they say no way am I going to work with this\n    \n    * The third type of user is one familiar with both and it\\'s easier for us to sell to latter two compared to the first one\n    \n    * First one is a cultural shift, not only a technology shift, so it is a slow process for the first time of user\n    \n    * The latter two types of users... they are the ones that work at organizations with bigger markets and money\n    \n    * Most of the people that are GIS specific they work in municipalities, cities... won\\'t pay a lot of money for software\n    \n    * Use free ArcGIS, ESIR licenses\n    \n    * Still want to target them but focusing more on the latter 2 and working on the cultural shift\n  * How do you think about product delivery, given a lot of open source tools in the market, how do you price, enough margin?\n    \n    * innovation in Sedona\n    \n    * one of our biggest innovation is in our product packaging and delivery\n    \n    * When we first started wherobots, first started working on an automated tool to put a version of sedona into their own environment\n    \n    * After we built that, threw it in the trash\n    \n    * The way we package the product today that provide a complete compute within any organization\n    \n    * For you to use wherobots today, you have to use it in the cloud and we fully manage and provision the compute for you, just focus on writing the code\n    \n    * Behind the scenes have IP for an engine that can take the code, compile it in efficient way\n    \n    * We recognize this doesn\\'t exist in a silo though, so want to create a whereobots data pipeline as part of a larger data workflow\n    \n    * Whatever it is... can create the outcome you want... can read it into ESRI and QGIS if you want\n    \n    * At the same time user removes the hurdle of maintaining the infrastructure\n    \n    * We price based on compute the user runs\n    \n    * Anyone can tomorrow wake up and decide they will charge based on compute, rent out EC2 cluster in AWS and then charge\n    \n    * We optimize the compute very well\n    \n    * Even with our profit margin added to this, still way cheaper than any other platform that exists out there for customers\n    \n    * Large profit margin 90\\%\n  * What stops folks from just doing this within Databricks\n    \n    * A lot of users are doing this within their databricks environment\n    \n    * Sedona in their own cloud data platforms\n    \n    * Multiple reasons not investing heavily into this... one of the biggest reasons is Gen AI\n    \n    * A lot of these data platforms there is an arms race to win gen AI\n    \n    * Everyone wants to make sure they don\\'t miss on this\n    \n    * And this in fact... i am happy with this because it gives us an opportunity to build and get ahead of the geospatial domain\n    \n    * Gives a little bit more time\n    \n    * The other reason is... few people on this planet that know both geospatial in one hand and data/ AI in another\n    \n    * Few technical people that understand both worlds very well\n    \n    * That is preventing some of the existing platforms like Snowflake from building something that is providing this first class citizen\n    \n    * Will be looking for... to acquire this talent\n    \n    * Look at the people working on geospatial at these companies\n    \n    * Want to solve this problem as a company... want to make this part of the cultural shift\n    \n    * Increase that number of people using geospatial\n  * It doesn\\'t feel like the goal is to build a new ESRI, it is to build a new databricks for the physical world\n    \n    * Juan you nailed it... we are not building ESRI.. wouldn\\'t mind if use ESRI at the same time\n    \n    * Integrations with folks like Felt, Mapbox others... makes a lot of sense where you sit\n  * Why start with geospatial and how do you expand from there?\n    \n    * There are series analysis, databases that exist out there\n    \n    * Are they good or no, can\\'t give you an answer\n    \n    * The one that was lacking was geospatial\n    \n    * And geospatial is also ubiquitous\n    \n    * We support spatial + X - X being traditional attributes that you present your data, time, image of the world\n    \n    * Everything happens somewhere and want to make sure the somewhere aspect is captured\n  * How do folks end up using the product in what interface? Or more just for processing in real-time embedded in platform\n* How do you guys think about traction and getting this to market, keen commercial eye ... where are you guys now and what are you optimizing for in the near-term\n  \n  * So far, the product is still an infant\n  \n  * Up to the end of 2024 were doing the build out phase\n  \n  * Building the MVP, the product we can sell\n  \n  * That said, we were selling while building\n  \n  * Have 12 paying customers today, small customers but they work with us closely, they report bugs and fix the bugs\n  \n  * Working with these customers today\n  \n  * Kind enough to give us marketing materials too\n  \n  * We love our early customers\n* Customer types - larger enterprise shifting to something new or is it startups? Just thinking who can put in touch with\n  \n  * We partition our ICP into different groups\n  \n  * The first horizon is we focused on what we called geospatial data producers... folks collecting a lot of geospatial data and producing a unified data base and they sell that data\n  \n  * Started with that first horizon focused on that first\n  \n  * Easiest to sell to, and easiest to adopt\n  \n  * Two horizons that come after, which includes big enterprise including infrastucture, energy, insurance, and automotive\n  \n  * Some using apache sedona today... these enterprises want to work with you if not SOC 2 compliant won\\'t buy\n  \n  * So right now fixing all these holes in the product to make sure these products can be sold\n  \n  * Just in terms of where going, will focus on horizon 1 but also looking to expand into next horizons\n  \n  * In terms of areas where we have seen a lot of interest... one is in renewable energy and energy infrastructure space\n  \n  * 9Zero is where they are based in SF (if ever in town love to have us there)\n  \n  * Lots of folks that have shared interest\n  \n  * Will be doing things for SF climate week, workshops\n  \n  * Telecom, Automotive, and other types of environmental assessment type workloads\n  \n  * Lot of interest in the financial services area for asset and/or risk pricing\n  \n  * Going through SOC 2 certification now\n* What is the objective of this meeting? What do we want to do next? We have been looking at this space for a long time and wanted to get in touch with the best entrepreneurs in this space. Fundraise?\n  \n  * Focus a lot on commercial viability, GTM, best way to deliver value for our investors is to back scalable companies - curious what doors we can open too\n  \n  * Just raised, not looking to raise right now... for good reasons\n  \n  * As we grow would want to work with partners that first, the most important thing for me is work with partners that believe in the vision\n  \n  * Building a company, there are a lot of ups and a lot of downs, believe in the vision\n  \n  * One thing you can help us with - if you can connect us to some of these potential customers in these domains, not everyone you bring to us will have commercial relationship but even 1-2 of them... help you study Wherobots too\n  \n  * If you want to take a decision to invest in the future, good to do this\n* When do you expect the next round and milestones before you get there?\n  \n  * Multiple, adoption and traction... have a lot on the open source, commercial product still infant... customers are using but want to grow customer base from 12 to 50-60 by end of 2025\n  \n  * Milestones related to revenue, # of monthly active customers, and the status of the product itself\n  \n  * That said, there is an exception to the rule, if there is a right partner we can trigger a round of financing earlier, this is an exception, and all about how working together will make a difference to Wherobots\n  \n  * Mid this year, September... might think of having a round if ton of alignment\n  \n  * Exception, not saying this is going to happen\n  \n  * But stay close in touch\n2025-01-15T09:52:19.784-08:00\n**Company Name: Wherobots**\n\n\n\nBrief description (one liner): The cloud-native spatial analytics platform.\n\n\n\n**Highlights:**\n\n* an INCREDIBLE vision to build a \\\"physical world first\\\" data lake structure\n\n* Super talented technical team with the critical mix of data and geospatial / physical expertise\n\n\n\nKey risks:\n\n* Need to build product beyond open source / MVP\n\n* Need to build and figure out commercialization\n\n* Need to understand budget and purchase patterns for target clients\n\n\n\n**PRODUCT**\n\n* PAIN POINT --> no data management, processing tools that treat \\\"physical data\\\" (geospatial, etc.) as a \\\"first class citizen\\\"\n  \n  * Focus has been on generic data, tables, slice & dice... snowflake, databricks, oracle\n  \n  * none of them treats the physical world -- which is a HUGE aspect of any data we collect today --\n* At Wherobots, they believe most of the data collected is from the physical world... so many signals collected on a second by second basis\n  \n  * Vision is to provide a platform for users to drive value out of this physical world data, easily, at scale, and with minimal friction\n    \n    * UNSTRUCTURED DATA\n    \n    * VOLUME\n    \n    * TIME SERIES\n  * Prior way to do it: take some data platform (snowflake, data bricks, etc.) \\\"DIY\\\" the geospatial capability in your own application\n    \n    * not efficient\n    \n    * not scalable\n    \n    * not easy to maintain\n  * Other solution: people use tools like ESRI to do geospatial analysis\n    \n    * good toolboxes, functionality\n    \n    * NO DATA MANAGEMENT, NO DATA PROCESSING CAPABILITY...\n      \n      * runs only in the application... so user has same limits to scale, cannot integrate with modern data infrastructure, completely silo\\'d\n      \n      * Doesn\\'t integrate with modern data and AI stack\n    * WHEROBOTS IS BRIDGING THE GAP!\n      \n      * Coming from modern data world\n      \n      * pushing physical world awareness inside the data lake platform\n      \n      * can get apps, build apps,\n* Reason they started:\n  \n  * Had a thesis at the university -- research on the two schools of thought, lots of benchmarks... came with prototype\n    \n    * Apache Sedona was the output of that... built as a protitype\n    \n    * Opensource tool took off... this was something the market really needed! 400k+ downloads\n  * Wanted to commercialize & build an ecosystem around it\n    \n    * Build an ecosystem that could help users make geospatial data easy and make buiding application liek a cakewalk\n      \n      * make geospatial app data pipeline\n      \n        * HOW TO DISRUPT THE CURRENT APPROACH\n\n**IMPACT**\n\n* MAKING GEOSPATIAL DATA ACCESSIBLE\n\n\n\n\n**MARKET**\n\n* Why now? ESRI NEEDS TO GO\n  \n  * 3 users\n    \n    Wherobots focused on both, but prefer to work with second and third ... deeper pockets :)\n    \n    * People went to school for GIS science, used ESRI, learned ESRI, can only\n    \n    * Traditional data engineer / data scientists that are working at other companeis, not GIS people, but data scientists!\n    \n    * User familiar with both\n    \n    * \n      \n      * GLUE missing between geospatial data and context / modern\n        \n        * have use cases... but don\\'t have geospatial intelligence / expertise\n        \n        * How do they price? INNOVATED MODEL / PACKAGING\n  \n  * Business model + product packaging / delivery ...\n  \n  * Cloud platform that provide fully managed, fully provisioned compute for geospatial\n  \n  * User just focuses on writing the code for application... no need to worry about scaling, etc.\n  \n  * IP for compiling code in most efficient way to proide most bang for buck so that they can run application on their own\n    \n    * Integrations with tools like AirFlow\n    \n    * How far \\\"up the stack\\\" do they go?\n    \n    * Price by ... volume of compute.\n      \n      * Given compute optimization... even with profit margin, 90\\%, still cheaper than everything out there!\n      \n      * GenAI arms race\n  \n  * Opportunity to build & get ahead for the geospatial domain until \\\"bigger people\\\" wake up\n  \n  * Very few technical people that know geospatial and data really well\n    \n    * Preventing some of the existing data platforms from building soemthing that is providing that \\'first class citizen\\' ... don\\'t have the talent\n    \n      * NOT BUILDING THE NEW ESRI... BUILDING THE NEW DATABRICKS FOR THE PHYSICAL WORLD\n  \n  * Provide automated tool that \\'ABSTRACTS AWAY\\' the complexity of these two bits... user can just bring context\n* Why start with geospatial?\n  \n  * More \\'foundational\\' / fundamental\n  \n  * Support \\\"Spatial plus X\\\" data processing --> traditional attributes, also time, image, whatever you\\'d like...\n* How is Go-To-Market motion uniquely suited?\n\n\n\n**TEAM**\n\n* Core team? Founders?\n  \n  * Mo: ex computer science professor at Arizona State (Apache Sedona)\n  \n  * Ben: OpenSource (Elastic Search, data team at Salesforce)\n* Founder-market fit?\n  \n  * Extremely high. Mo literally wrote the Apache Sedona, shows good commercial instincts.\n  \n  * Founder-Energize fit?\n  \n  * Extremely high.\n  \n  * Gaps in management team?\n  \n  * Sales?\n  \n  \n\n\nBASED IN NINE ZERO IN SAN FRANCISCO\n\n* Will be doing something for SF Climate Week ... we should def do something with them\n\n\n\n\n**ECONOMICS**\n\n* Traction? tons in open source, but commercial product in infancy\n\n* Products \\\"still an infant\\\"\n\n* Prior to 2024: focus was buildout for team, building MVP\n  \n  * Selling while building\n  \n  * 12 paying customers today... small customers, but they work with Wherobots, run workloads, give feedback\n  \n  * Mostly large orgs or mostly small orgs?\n  \n  * ICP segmentation\n    \n    * Geospatial data producers -- collecting a lot of GIS data, produccing a unified dataset of addresses, land parcels, generate point of interest data, overture maps,\n      \n      * Focus here --> easiest to sell to, easiest to provide value\n      \n          * Bigger enterprise -- infra, energy, real estate, insurance, automotive\n      \n      * Some using Apache Sedona today, but didn\\'t focus because they tend to need SOC2 compliance, more complex MVPs\n      \n      * What KPIs show growth / strength?\n\n* If available, cash efficiency / go-to-market efficiency data?\n\n\n\n**DEAL**\n\n* Expected round size / timing?\n  \n  * MID SEPT THIS YEAR\n    \n    * Round likely triggered \\\"early\\\"... for the right partner ... :)\n    \n    * What milestones will this round allow us to achieve?\n  \n  * From 12 to 50-60 customers by EOY 2025\n  \n  * Product moving beyond MVP\n2024-08-12T07:47:39.491-07:00\nReached out on email, linkedin, through max Nova, and through ASu... no luck so far.",
