         "2025-07-22T10:42:32.595-07:00\nPassed:\n\n* Low ACVs ~$30K\n\n* High churn and low NRR (~85\\%)\n2025-07-15T12:22:15.382-07:00\nAttendees: Genna, Pete, Ana, Agus\n\n \n\nTLDR: Raising a Series A. At $1.6M in ARR, Australia/NZ & Europe. Solution to model assets at the utility and C&I scale.\n\n\n\nNotes:\n\n* Intro\n  \n  * Genna\n    \n    * CCO\n    \n    * Been with Gridcog a couple of year\n    \n    * Joined since they expanded into Europe\n    \n    * Power trading background\n    \n    * Battery optimizer of GB (before got acquired by Shell), and worked on the fleet\n  * Pete\n    \n    * Cofounder and CPO at Gridcog\n    \n    * Originally from Britain, but also spend time in Australia\n    \n    * Spent a lot of time in the energy space\n    \n    * Then turned into the tech world\n    \n    * They did a first startup in the energy spce, and then they got acquired by Shell and worked a couple of year with them\n    \n    * That\\'s when they had the idea\n* Genesis\n  \n  * Gridcog founded during the pandemic\n  \n  * Energy market on one side and capacity market on the other\n  \n  * Then they started to get excited to expand beyond\n* Problem\n  \n  * ICP - utility scale project developer\n    \n    * They start with start selection and initial feasibility, and geo selection\n    \n    * All the way down to final investment decision\n  * Helps them decied\n    \n    * What is AC vs DC capacity\n    \n    * How much flex to include\n  * Historically this would come from yeild \\* price\n    \n    * Plexos and Baringa\n    \n    * Q \\* P\n* Product\n  \n  * Now flex brings a new dimension to the equation around bankability\n    \n    * This is for a particular ICP around utility scale\n    \n      * For Amazon, they are doing EV charging modular, they need to put more EV chargers in a site, and you don\\'t have enough grid connection, so  there are tricks to that\n    \n    * Co-locating with battery storage\n    \n    * Operationg management and charging of the fleet\n  * **Bankable dispatch is the common ground of all these use cases**\n  \n  * **Solution**\n    \n    * They are modeling the grid connection\n    \n    * Then they are modeling the large and small batteries\n    \n    * Universals markets model > how do you represent all the energy market and ancilliary services around the project. This is key for mostly C&I\n    \n    * They incorporate the PPA models\n    \n    * Modeling a project and then they run many different simulations\n* Customer\n  \n  * Split very evenly between large and small\n    \n    * Distribution networks modeling fleet electrificaiton\n    \n    * Large utility solar modeling batteries\n  * Many types\n    \n    * Customer majors\n    \n    * Large energy users\n    \n    * Asset developers\n  * Also many types of assets\n    \n    * Batteires\n    \n    * Solar\n    \n    * Evs\n  * The buyers and users and very similar across the board\n    \n    * Energy analysts\n    \n    * The persona is so consistent\n    \n    * Onboarding them into the software is very similar\n* GTM\n  \n  * Most of their leads are inbound\n  \n  * They have a very lean team, they just brought in new\n* Business model / Pricing\n  \n  * SaaS with different subscription tiers\n    \n    * Based on the number of projects\n    \n    * Number of asset types you are using\n    \n    * Number of energy markets you are modeling in\n  * The last one is were they pushed ACV\n* Geographies\n  \n  * \\\"Supported markets\\\", they have an industry team and they are very deep expertise there\n  \n  * Australia, NZ, GB, France etc.\n  \n  * The customers are using the model to actually model in China\n* Are you in the hook? How do you manage missmatch of expecations\n  \n  * Transparency purely\n  \n  * They give the clients all of the data and assumptions\n  \n  * \\\"Aurora is legendary for the lack of transparency\\\"\n* Data\n  \n  * They do not genreate price forecasts\n  \n  * They use their own tooling, and the customer can bring data too\n  \n  * If customers want forecasts they bring it themselves, and some customers have their own forecasting teams internally\n  \n  * They don\\'t intent to do price forecasting, customers want a tool that is flexible and let them add whatever data they want\n  \n  * You can model against a bullish and bareish models\n* Simulation\n  \n  * They spend a lot of time around modeling against live assets\n  \n  * They are harvesting data out of operating and real assets\n  \n  * To make the modeling and simulation as accurate as possible\n  \n  * They run a live digital asset in parallel to a physical asset, to backtest assumptions and be more specific\n* Fundraising\n  \n  * Last raise\n    \n    * Was all about getting out of Australia\n    \n      * This raise\n    \n    * Conquering Europe\n    \n    * Series A size of 8-10M\n    \n    * Access on the data room\n* Traction\n  \n  * Q4 \\'23 $0.6M\n  \n  * Q4 \\'24 $1M\n  \n  * Q2 \\'25 $1.7M\n  \n  * Logos: Shell, Total, pwc, Amazon, Zestec, Nissan\n  \n  * June was a record month for them\n  \n  * Q2 was really good\n  \n  * Doubled revenue in Europe in the last 6 months\n2025-07-15T07:21:27.036-07:00\nAttendees: Pete, Genna, Agus, Ana\n\n\n\nTLDR: Building a modeling tool for flexible assets across both distributed and grid scale. Raising $8-10m Series A. Have seen solid growth in EU recent quarters, but was a slow start. Take a look at data room & discuss\n\n\n\nNotes:\n\n* Introductions\n  \n  * Genna Chief Commercial Officer, been with Gridcog a couple of years now, joined as started to build out operations in Europe\n  \n  * Prior to Gridcog was at Limejump, set up trading desk, and commercial teams\n  \n  * Pete - co-foudner and chief product officer at Gridcog, originally a brit\n  \n  * Splitting time between EU and Australia... spending most of my time around perth\n  \n  * Fabian - big data and analytics for C&I, exited to shell\n  \n  * What we saw was a business that was fascinated by flex, wanted to invest in it, but couldn\\'t figure out how to make projects pencil\n  \n  * Price x volume doesn\\'t make any sense\n  \n  * Gridcog was founded in mid-2025 when everyone else was making sourdough\n  \n  * Originally in Australia, globally lab rat, useful market on which to build a product like ours\n  \n  * Couple years ago got serious about growth outside of Aus\n  \n  * Jenna was first employee in London and now that part of the world is half our team and more than half revenue\n  \n  * Double ARR in UK just this half of the year\n  \n  * Last raise was about getting out of Aus, now this raise is about conquering Europe\n* Product\n  \n  * Will send deck + demo\n  \n  * Energy project modeling software for the energy transition, particualrly focused on flex\n  \n  * Large scale or small, and we think it\\'s complex and challenging to model using the existing tools and processes\n  \n  * Doubled growth out of UK in YTD\n  \n  * Growth in Renewables creates instability, non-firm ad this needs balancing\n  \n  * Load flexibility, BESS, EV and fleet charging, curtailable renewable generation, clean gas generation\n  \n  * Split between larger and small scale\n  \n  * Have clients modeling very large flexible assets\n  \n  * Flex modeling is more complex than traditional solar and wind modeling\n  \n  * Project development goes across the value chain but has focused less on the earlier stage\n  \n  * Existing distressed solar farm, so starting to think about what do they do about it, when do they install storage, should they DC couple, AC couple, meat and potatos for software like ours\n  \n  * Historically a lot of modeling has been PVSyst (yield)\n  \n  * Flex introduced a new dynamic to this is bankable dispatch, which is to say have some pricing and steerable asset but is a dumb brick until ask them to do something and why\n  \n  * This is for utility-scale project developer, Amazon or Shell in Europe doing fleet electrification\n  \n  * Energetic feasibility, hundreds of depots that only have skinny grid connection\n  \n  * 11 year queue, co-locating battery storage as a buffer as wait for queue\n  \n  * There\\'s so many similarities we are modeling the grid conenction on that side, and variations on the site\n  \n  * In the software we have parameterized a battery\n  \n  * THen we got the commercial model, and a key component of that is the universal model\n  \n  * Lego bricks to reflect the different components of wholesale market and energy services\n  \n  * So the end of the day, what clients are doing in gridcog, they are bringing in data to our model, reflecting the physical aspects of the site, what markets they\\'re in, commercial aspects running a simulation, form part of a large mathematical problem of 1000 different variations have done\n  \n  * Although coming from different\n  \n  * In some cases and distributed small scale flex will absolutely compete large scale\n  \n  * Can build 1 kw on a roof the same for ina field\n  \n  * Very bullish distributed flex, very complicated\n  \n  * Original focus was on behind the meter\n  \n  * Inbound from large scale folks\n* Commercially covering a lot, how do manage\n  \n  * They can be different customer types from logo themselves\n  \n  * The personal we are selling to, our buyer and users are incredible similar across the board\n  \n  * Energy analyst is someone with engineering & finance degree\n  \n  * And this is so consistent across the different ICPs\n  \n  * And then when it comes to the buyer usually speaking it is the head of that team\n  \n  * We\\'ve h ad a lot of success in building brand, and most really strong logos have come from marketing led activities\n  \n  * Saying hey here is an interesting thing happening in the market, but to date most of leads are inbound\n  \n  * Brought on first sales hires into team 8 mo ago\n  \n  * One in GB focusing on behind the meter project developer to really understand this buyer\n* Pricing model\n  \n  * SaaS based, different subscription tiers ddepending on 3 levers\n  \n  * \\# of projectd modeling per month\n  \n  * Number of asset types using\n  \n  * And the biggest one that has driven expansion, is # of markets modeling in, GB to France, Germany...\n  \n  * This is where we have seen a shift to higher ACVs in Europe\n  \n  * Start with a team modeling in 1 energy markets\n* How many energy markets currently covering and how easy to model new market?\n  \n  * 30 countries globally, but to do that leveraging universal markets\n  \n  * Some markets we have industry team and we deeply understand what is going on\n  \n  * Where our clients have expert users\n* How much are you on the hook for performance\n  \n  * Transparency in the simulation\n  \n  * Utility scale project development\n  \n  * Aurora, old guard, they are legendary for lack of transparency\n  \n  * We go the other way, you can see what you can put into models, document a lot of the mathematical techniques\n  \n  * Clients hit up the industry team, doing financial due diligence\n  \n  * CLients trying to get debt financing, scenario based tool, take a bearish model, but other cases can show the upside, rarely single project variation that a client is considering\n* Exteral products for price forecast\n  \n  * Clients can bring inputs and assumptions\n  \n  * A part of the product, if you want to run backcasting models on bullish and bearish year\n  \n  * We provide that and our clients can mechanically transform that, if clients want a forecast can bring themselves\n  \n  * Otherwise, they will buy curves from everyone, we see everyone\\'s curves\n  \n  * One of the Aurora curves wrong within 180\\% within a month\n  \n  * Don\\'t intend to do market priced forecasting, want a tool that allows them to bring their own curves and bring their own\n  \n  * We spend a ton of time benchmarking a ton of time against real world assets\n  \n  * Next project is tracking, all about live digital commercial twin of deployed assets\n  \n  * Back to the well, not dissimilar to original business, yesterday asset made 10 EUR, optimal was 30\n\n* Traction\n  \n  * $1.7M in ARR, most growth coming from UK\n  \n  * Fundraise\n  \n  * $8-10M fundraise\n2025-03-05T15:31:30.134-08:00\nAttendees: Fabian, Ana\n\n\n\nTLDR: It seems like they\\'re building the Tyba project sim solution, but for behind the meter as well. Focused in EU markets. Might be conflicted out but KIT ahead of Series A later this year.\n\n\n\nNotes:\n\n* Overview\n  \n  * Our big focus at the moment in Europe\n  \n  * Broadly on the landscape\n  \n  * Part of the way we think about it...\n  \n  * Sometimes we talk about breaking into market into two halves... two that are network centric like transmission/ distributuin (grid tech, orchestration)\n  \n  * We\\'re on the other side, the assets markets using the network\n  \n  * Such a different motion\n  \n  * Sit at this intersection of the grid and the end assets\n* ICP\n  \n  * Core customer profile is some kind of asset developer or asset owner... and that could be a large energy users who are self-developing their own site\n  \n  * Behind the meter developer, front of the meter developer\n  \n  * Anyone is trying to invest in the energy transition, how to make project decisions, how big things should be\n  \n  * Then we have customers in the supply chain like optimizers, battery vendors, consultants\n  \n  * Primariyl our focus is that long-term asset owner and is tring to navigate some project decision\n  \n  * And what we do that is hard\n  \n  * Modeling projects for multiple types of assets\n  \n  * Project swhere those have some sort of route to market\n  \n  * And being able to do both those things in a flexible way\n  \n  * Behind the meter it is our heritage particularly in Australia\n  \n  * What is interesting about behind the meter projects complexity comes from co-location -- load + other resources together\n  \n  * Solar farm owner, lot of strnaded assets, integrate storage to these\n* Idea is to make this for financial decisions\n  \n  * We want to stay in commercial analysis, don\\'t want to move into optimization\n  \n  * We want to focus on the indepenent commercial analysis layer, want to span the entire asset life cycle\n  \n  * Beta product called tracking, taking asset telemetry, shadow optimizing the assets\n  \n  * Comparing model performance because as an asset owner\n* Modo\n  \n  * We know the Modo guys well, lot of their benchmarking but a lot of what we\\'re doing is private data from asset owner\n  \n  * The benchmark, model asset performed a bit better or worse and understand the why around that\n  \n  * Asset owners will go out every couple of years, will re-tender their portfolio, do this analysis in shadow optimization internally in those procurement processes\n  \n  * Planning and pre-investment decision is more our focus\n* Financing partners\n  \n  * We are involved in this\n  \n  * if you are a front of the meter developer... will depend on debt funding to fund your project, this is where Modo is focused\n  \n  * Baringa, Aurora Energy Research\n  \n  * Do these services for independent revenue studies\n  \n  * But when you look at that investment decision across the full spectrum of projects involved in\n  \n  * It\\'s not all in the debt funding side...\n  \n  * Must more on equity and balance sheet stage... role is keeping project development team doo decisions in site selection\n  \n  * Do want to move into bankability as well\n  \n  * One of our developer customers is a German developer developing in Poland\n  \n  * Wanted to understand the market a bit better\n* Some of the modeling\n  \n  * it can be multi-hour\n  \n  * Part of the philosophy around the software... lots of scenarios and backtesting\n  \n  * What might look like one asset could look like a few hundred\n  \n  * Modeling is the variations in the data and the granularity but the big hard thing doing, solving optimization program\n  \n  * optimizer has some sort of planning window\n  \n  * We do the same thing when we\\'re running valutions\n  \n  * Digital twin or replica of what an optimizer is doing\n  \n  * So that is where some of that computational work comes from\n  \n  * Tat said, have project models that will run in 1-2 minutes\n  \n  * But we do see more complicated modes being multi-hour\n* project simulation and simulation side (talking about Tyba overlap)\n  \n  * Very generic market model\n  \n  * Being a global business, have to be able to operate in any market\n  \n  * Each new market, build out local team and build bespoke there\n  \n  * But part of our advantage is our software is used in lots of markets\n  \n  * Romania, Poland... Croatia...\n  \n  * The reason we can do that, giving people a tool and they can make decisions on how they use it\n  \n  * To give you an example... regulated frequency on the grid, is one of the big things for batteries\n  \n  * GB is it dynamic suite\n  \n  * One model it is different pricing data\n  \n  * Flexible generating\n  \n  * Shell -- even though nothing in US model, modeling projects in PJM...\n  \n  * They can embed in their own data into the platform\n  \n  * But then if you are in the US can run\n* Why Europe\n  \n  * Europe is much more homogenous from a power markets perspective\n  \n  * It was easier to prove that out in Europe because less fragmented than in the US\n  \n  * Each country has different rules, but because it\\'s the EU they have a lot of homogenization\n  \n  * Some things that change as you move between countries\n  \n  * That meant.. much easier for us\n  \n  * More of Europe is that classic de-regulated market\n  \n  * Whereas the US has some quite different market structures\n  \n  * Moving to homogenize the rules in EU as well\n* Priorities\n  \n  * The big focus for us right now, last year we were founder-led sales\n  \n  * Big thing we\\'re doing right now is building out GTM team separate from business\n  \n  * Moving to SaaS enterprise model, before we were mostly inbound\n  \n  * THat\\'s... in the weeks of running the startuo is leveling that up\n  \n  * In paralell to that moving from GB to intercontinental Europe\n  \n  * Germany and Benelux\n  \n  * In the process of setting up German subsidiary\n  \n  * French and Germans want to switch to their language\n  \n  * That is our challenge right now\n* Fundraise\n  \n  * Series A in second half of the year\n2024-07-23T16:56:54.551-07:00\nAttendees: Fabian, Ana\n\n\n\nTLDR: Aim to end the year at $1.94M, currently at ~$1M. They build the development product for C&I applications, including fleets. They\\'re like a Tyba for C&I. Most revenues concentrated in Australia and UK but plan to expand to the US in the coming years. KIT\n\n\n\nNotes\n\n* Overview\n  \n  * Fabian - computer scientist originally\n  \n  * Had earlier startup for C&I energy usage\n  \n  * Sold to a retailer then aquired by Shell energy\n  \n  * One of the challenges for the power sectors challenges is selling solutions which is why they bought us\n  \n  * GridCog is a modeling business, modeling all sort of projects, micro-grids, battery energy storage projects... anything interacting with power markets\n* Product follow-ups\n  \n  * Product overviews\n    \n    * Accurately modeling particularly for behind the meter projects how they can particiapte in the energy markets\n    \n    * Can have fleets and see how they interact with energy markets\n  * C&I or utility\n    \n    * Majority projects modeled in the software in C&I\n    \n    * Lines behind and front of meter are being blurred is because of co-location\n    \n    * Co-location brings a lot of complexity\n    \n    * The other reason lines are being blurred seeing a lot of innovation in energy markets where behind the meter assets are participating in energy\n  * Do just the modeling or also connect to commands once operations\n    \n    * Product focus is pre-investment or pre-project modeling\n    \n    * Emerging product on the tracking side, how do we provide the lens on the commercial performance of the asset, delivered value of the project against model\n    \n    * One of the trends we see in the UK and Europe, if you are people are setting up shadow trading teams to monitor the performance of their assets, understand how the third part is performing, and this seems to be a manual process\n  * Asset types most commonly serve\n    \n    * Broadly we don\\'t do standalone solar or wind\n    \n    * Good solutions for that\n    \n    * Almost always a battery involved\n    \n    * Fleets too - they are interesting, how do you optimize charging schedules\n    \n    * Sweet spot is probably where there is some market value and mid-scale assets, co-located solar assets + storage\n    \n    * Sub 5 MW scale\n    \n    * Industrial and commercial solutions\n    \n    * There are interested fleet solutions out there for a while like scheduling and rosters\n    \n    * How do we think about the interaction with the energy system\n    \n    * 50 buses, 10 trucks here is the schedule, when we run our simulations can come up with optimal charging scenarios, can model out V2G examples\n  * So more like workflow tool?\n    \n    * Yes, the reason we started to do front of the meter\n    \n    * They were buying two products from consultants, project modeling and the forward curves\n    \n    * But if you\n    \n    * Still need to bank the project through the consultant, but a large part of the dev project can do the model yourself vs. going back to consultants for 30K per ask ... can just go once instead of repeatedly as the project specifications change\n  * Work directly with end users in C&I?\n    \n    * Work with EY and PWC so don\\'t work directly with large energy users\n    \n      * Consulting type model?\n    \n    * We do find we end up providing advice to people\n    \n    * The way we do that is via a chat interface in the software\n    \n    * Technical advice\n* Customer\n  \n  * All the big energy suppliers in C&I\n  \n  * Some residential ones for residential\n  \n  * And also project developers\n  \n  * Consultants/ advisors\n  \n  * December of last year led by UK investor, 6 people in the UK now\n  \n  * People like Shell modeling their EV charging business\n  \n  * Anyone who is trying to understand the economics and commerciality of investment decisions\n* Markets serve\n  \n  * Australia, NZ, UK, and Europe and have one Japanese customer that is operating in US\n  \n  * We provide access to historical markets data, all back-casting markets\n  \n  * Can go to their party markets\n  \n  * Can set up their own forward curves\n  \n  * Neutral or agnostic to power price curves, not very good, no one really knows what is going on, forward curves is not where to compete\n  \n  * If you are a proponent of a project want to have a bullish case for your project, self inflating project, sometimes people are over optimistic\n  \n  * In the UK, to where the forward curves, $150K per MW but down to 50K this year\n  \n  * Our perspective you should own your models & data, it is\n* Competitors\n  \n  * Modo energy - they aren\\'t able to say what the real revenue performance of the assets is because they only have visibility into the public markets data, and they are not modeling specific assets, not shadow optimizing, just collecting market data\n  \n  * Some people in the solar space, like PVCase, Helioscope\n  \n  * Homer is the traditional\n  \n  * Zendi (modern web based solution of Homer)\n  \n  * We focus a lot more on energy markets and how you address\n  \n  * They focus more on the cost side of things\n  \n  * Energy Exemplar - Plexos, they are like the dominant player for system market model to come up with power price curves\n  \n  * We are a more modern/ accessible product than Plexos, also Plexos expensive\n  \n  * Energy Toolbase -- started with solar, don\\'t handle complexity as well\n* Traction\n  \n  * Now trying to report our numbers in pounds\n  \n  * 800K pounds in ARR\n  \n  * Want to end the year at 1.5 pounds in ARR\n  \n  * As of today, 70\\% Australia, and then the balance in the UK team\n  \n  * Want to get to 50:50 by the end of the year\n* Fundraise\n  \n  * Seed 3.3M pounds\n  \n  * Thinking probably the next raise will be linked to entry in a new market, probably in 12-18 months\n2023-08-24T12:25:00.190-07:00\nAENU connection / offered intro",
